    <div class="shell sk-wrapper" data-document-layout={documentLayout} data-size="lg">
      {/*
        The rail is the component, enhanced: `data-sk-sidebar` is what runs the resize behaviour over
        the authored markup. It has no trigger and does not want one, the index is the page's
        furniture and collapsing it to a rail of icons would leave nothing readable; the enhancer
        accepts either control, so a resize-only sidebar is a legal one.

        `data-storage-key` is what makes the width outlive the reload, which matters more here than
        in most consumers: every link is a full navigation, so a width that reset on each page would
        be a width nobody could keep.
      */}
      <aside
        class="sk-sidebar docs-sidebar"
        data-sk-sidebar
        data-storage-key="docs-rail"
      >
      {/*
        THE SKIP LINK'S DESTINATION, and `tabindex="-1"` is what makes it one.

        Following an in-page link scrolls in every browser but moves FOCUS in only some of them.
        Where it does not, the next Tab resumes from the link, which lands the reader back in the
        header they just asked to skip: a skip link that silently does nothing is worse than no skip
        link, because the reader was told it worked. `-1` takes it out of the tab ORDER while making
        it a focus target, so nothing else about tabbing changes.

